\textbf{Outlook.}
A natural next step is evaluation on empirical social networks~\cite{yin2017higherorder} and geographically explicit synthetic contact networks~\cite{jiang2024synthetic}, with communities defined by organizational membership or place of residence. Available node-level connections would provide both a full-network reference and aggregated intercommunity weights $W_{ij}$. A complementary approach is to construct local contact networks from demographic data and connect communities using mobility-informed weights~\cite{tizzoni2014mobility}, with explicit assumptions linking travel flows to contact intensities. Initial studies could use static network aggregates; retaining contact times would require a temporal-network extension. Such studies should examine sensitivity to community definitions, contact weights, disease parameters, and incomplete observations, while distinguishing approximation error from uncertainty in network construction. This evaluation warrants a dedicated follow-up study; validation against observed spreading outcomes would require additional evidence.

